## DOMAIN 2: DATA STORE MANAGEMENT (26%)

### S3 Storage Class Decision

| Access Pattern | Storage Class |
|----------------|---------------|
| Accessed daily/frequently | S3 Standard |
| Accessed < 1x per month | S3 Standard-IA |
| Unpredictable access pattern | S3 Intelligent-Tiering |
| Archive (minutes retrieval) | Glacier Instant Retrieval |
| Archive (hours retrieval) | Glacier Flexible Retrieval |
| Deep archive (12+ hours OK) | Glacier Deep Archive |
| Single-AZ, infrequent, reproducible | One Zone-IA |

**Key Rules:**
- Frequent access + retrieval fees = expensive → use Standard
- "Rarely accessed" ≠ Glacier (if instant retrieval needed → Standard-IA)
- Lifecycle policies automate transitions

### Redshift Distribution Styles

| Style | When to Use |
|-------|-------------|
| KEY | Join column with HIGH cardinality (millions of unique values) |
| ALL | Small dimension tables (< 100K rows) — copied to every node |
| EVEN | No clear join pattern, or when KEY causes skew |
| AUTO | Let Redshift decide (good default) |

**Skew Fix:** If KEY distribution is skewed (one value = 40% of data) → change to a HIGHER-CARDINALITY column (not EVEN — you lose join optimization)

### Redshift Sort Keys

| Type | Best For | Rule |
|------|----------|------|
| Compound | ONE dominant filter column | Must filter LEFT-TO-RIGHT in order |
| Interleaved | Multiple equally-used filter columns | Any column combo works |

**Compound Sort Key Rule (PHONE BOOK ANALOGY):**
```
Key: (A, B, C)
WHERE A = ?              ✅ Uses sort key
WHERE A = ? AND B = ?    ✅ Uses sort key
WHERE B = ?              ❌ Skipped A — USELESS
WHERE A = ? OR B = ?     ❌ OR breaks sort key
ORDER BY B               ❌ Not globally sorted by B alone
```

**Trade-off:** Interleaved needs VACUUM REINDEX (higher maintenance)

### Redshift COPY Best Practices
- Split files so count = multiple of slices (e.g., 8 slices → 8, 16, 24 files)
- GZIP files CANNOT be split for parallel loading — split before compressing
- Single large GZIP file = slow (only one slice can read it)
- Use manifest files for explicit file lists

### Redshift Materialized Views
- `AUTO REFRESH YES` → automatic refresh, zero effort
- NOT enabled by default — must explicitly configure
- Redshift decides when to refresh based on data changes

### Redshift Data Sharing
- Share data across Redshift clusters/accounts WITHOUT copying
- Producer creates datashare → Consumer queries directly
- Cross-account and cross-region supported

### Redshift Enhanced VPC Routing
- Forces ALL Redshift traffic (COPY, UNLOAD, Spectrum) through VPC
- Required when "traffic must not traverse public internet"
- Combine with VPC Gateway Endpoint for S3

### Partitioning vs Bucketing

| Technique | When to Use | Example |
|-----------|-------------|---------|
| Partition | Low cardinality (< 1000 values) | date, region, status |
| Bucket | High cardinality (millions of values) | customer_id, user_id |

**Partition Explosion Warning:**
- 365 days × 24 hours × 200 countries = 1.75M partitions/year → TOO MANY
- Solution: Bucket on high-cardinality columns instead

### Athena Partition Projection
- Eliminates Crawler/ALTER TABLE dependency
- Athena calculates partitions mathematically (no Glue Catalog lookup)
- Best for: predictable, sequential partitions (daily dates, hourly)
- Handles millions of partitions without enumeration overhead

### DynamoDB Key Design

| Access Pattern | Key Design |
|----------------|------------|
| Get items by owner, sorted by time | PK: owner_id, SK: timestamp |
| Get specific item by ID | PK: item_id |
| Query by non-key attribute | Create GSI on that attribute |

**GSI vs LSI:**
- GSI: Different partition key, can be added anytime
- LSI: Same partition key, different sort key, must be created at table creation

### DynamoDB On-Demand Scaling
- Scales to **2x previous peak** instantly
- Beyond 2x within 30 minutes → THROTTLES
- 10x spike from zero → WILL throttle
- For predictable large spikes → use provisioned + auto-scaling

### Small Files Problem
- Cause: Many tiny files (< 128 MB) in S3
- Impact: Slow Athena/Spark queries (overhead per file)
- Fix: Compaction job (Glue/EMR) to merge into 128-512 MB files
- Prevention: Firehose buffer size, Spark coalesce/repartition

### Data Lake Upserts / SCD Type 2
- Always use **Apache Hudi** or **Delta Lake**
- Supports: merge, upsert, delete, time-travel
- SCD Type 2: track history with effective start/end dates
- Copy-on-Write (CoW): fast reads, slow writes
- Merge-on-Read (MoR): fast writes, slower reads (good for high-update tables)

### File Formats

| Format | Best For |
|--------|----------|
| Parquet | Columnar queries (SELECT few columns from many) |
| ORC | Similar to Parquet (Hive ecosystem) |
| Avro | Schema evolution, row-based processing |
| JSON | Raw/landing zone, human-readable |
| CSV | Legacy, simple flat data |

**Parquet advantage:** Column pruning — only reads columns you SELECT (huge cost/performance savings with Athena)

